Package: merya
Type: Package
Title: Fast BCa Bootstrap Confidence Intervals and CI-Inversion P-Values
    for Common Statistical Tests and Models
Version: 0.9.0
Authors@R: person(given = "Your", family = "Name",
    email = "you@example.com", role = c("aut", "cre"))
Description: Drop-in replacements for stats::t.test(), stats::cor.test(),
    stats::lm(), and stats::glm() that report bias-corrected and
    accelerated (BCa) bootstrap confidence intervals and
    confidence-interval-inversion p-values instead of (or alongside) the
    classical asymptotic results. Functions take the same arguments and
    return objects of the same classes ("htest", "lm", "glm") as their
    original counterparts, so existing code and downstream methods keep
    working. boot.lm(), boot.glm(), and boot.t.test() default to a wild
    bootstrap, which keeps the design matrix fixed and perturbs each
    observation's own residual rather than resampling rows, with the
    classical resample-the-rows-with-replacement bootstrap remaining
    available as an option; every bootstrap-based function additionally
    accepts a choice of BCa or plain percentile confidence intervals,
    applied uniformly regardless of resampling method. boot.t.test() is
    internally reformulated as a regression so that it agrees exactly
    with boot.lm() for the same outcome and a binary predictor, given the
    same settings. boot.lm() can additionally report, for each predictor,
    its partial correlation, partial eta-squared, or eta-squared (with
    confidence intervals and CI-inversion p-values) instead of its slope
    coefficient, and optionally a bootstrapped, leave-one-out predicted
    R-squared for the model as a whole. boot.glm() can report odds
    ratios, conditional or marginal (g-computation) risk ratios, marginal
    risk differences, or the population attributable fraction, in place
    of its raw coefficients. A single generic, boot.ci(), also takes an
    object already produced by t.test(), cor.test(), lm(), or glm() and
    returns it with the same bootstrap inference substituted in, using
    the exact same internal computation as the corresponding boot.*()
    function. All bootstrap-based functions default to 10000 resamples
    and to a fixed seed of 123 for reproducibility (settable to any other
    integer, or to NULL to use whatever random state is currently active
    instead of reseeding). The bootstrap resampling, BCa transform, and
    CI-inversion p-value machinery is implemented entirely in base R
    (using only the 'stats' package that ships with every R installation,
    with no other dependencies); resampling is vectorized wherever a
    closed-form statistic allows it -- the wild bootstrap in particular
    needs no explicit loop over replicates at all, since the design
    matrix is fixed across replicates -- and the BCa transform is
    inverted analytically for p-values (no bisection / root-finding),
    which keeps the bootstrap and p-value calculations fast even for
    large numbers of replicates.
